<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Mrauk U Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1429 CE – 1785 CE · capital Mrauk U</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The coastal kingdom of Arakan that ruled from the temple city of Mrauk U, mixed Buddhist kingship with Bengal Sultanate styles and dominated the Bay of Bengal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/min-saw-mon">Min Saw Mon</a>, the exiled last king of the Lemro period of the Kingdom of Arakan, regained his throne in 1429 with an army from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate</a> and in 1430 founded a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mrauk-u">Mrauk U</a>. For a time Arakan paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, and its Buddhist kings took Persian-style Muslim titles and issued coins with Persian inscriptions.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/min-bin">Min Bin</a> in the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mrauk-u">Mrauk U</a> became independent and powerful, controlling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> and raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> with fleets that included Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. Slaves captured in Bengal worked its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> lands. The capital's stone temples, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shitthaung-temple">Shitthaung</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koe-thaung-temple">Koe-thaung</a>, survive. Its court hosted the Bengali poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaol">Alaol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulat-qazi">Daulat Qazi</a>, and Muslim and Buddhist officials served together.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> in 1666, and internal instability followed. In 1784 to 1785 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> conquered Arakan and carried off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahamuni-buddha">Mahamuni Buddha</a>. Refugee flows into British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> helped trigger the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mrauk-u-kingdom.jpg" alt="Mrauk U Kingdom" width="320" height="414"><figcaption>Image: Beylarbey · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Mrauk_U_in_the_1630s.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1429 CE – 1785 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mrauk U</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Min Saw Mon, Min Bin, Thiri Thudhamma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arakanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>, <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Mrauk U, Arakan, Kingdom of Arakan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1607&amp;focus=mrauk-u-kingdom" aria-label="Mrauk U Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7189 1416 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mrauk U Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7509 1672L7511 1673M7509 1672L7339 1518M7509 1672L7576 1622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7511 1673h0M7339 1518h0M7576 1622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7509 1672h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7509 1672h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mrauk U. <a href="/atlas?year=1607&amp;focus=mrauk-u-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
